CH = dict(num=11, title="Keeping Time with the Skies", theme="Moon phases, calendars, satellites",
 intro="The Moon sometimes shines in the daytime and changes shape from day to day, while the Sun, seasons and Moon give us natural **repeating cycles**. This chapter explains **why the Moon has phases**, where to look for it, how the **day, month and year** led to **lunar, solar and luni-solar calendars**, how festivals follow them, and why we launch **artificial satellites**.",
 big_questions=["Why is the Moon sometimes visible when the Sun is up?", "What would a day, month or year mean on the Moon?", "How could time be measured without clocks or calendars?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "11.1 How Does the Moon’s Appearance Change, and Why?"),
 ("steps", [
   ("Activity 11.1 — Watch the Moon for a month", "Start at sunrise on the day after full Moon (Moon in the **west**). Each day record the date, whether seen at sunrise or sunset, shade the bright portion, note if it increased or decreased and whether it is closer to or farther from the Sun. After ~15 days it cannot be seen at sunrise, so observe at **sunset** for the next 15 days."),
   ("What you find", "The shape differs each day, the Moon is not visible on all days, and its position in the sky relative to the Sun shifts day by day.")]),
 ("h3", "11.1.1 Phases of the Moon"),
 ("ul", ["From full Moon the bright part shrinks to a half in about a week and disappears in another week — the **waning period** (**Krishna Paksha**).",
         "After the new Moon the bright part grows to half in a week and to full in another — the **waxing period** (**Shukla Paksha**).",
         "**Full Moon day = Purnima** (full bright circle); **new Moon day = Amavasya** (not visible).",
         "From one full Moon to the next takes about a month. The changing shape of the bright portion seen from Earth is called the **phases of the Moon**."]),
 ("table", ["Phase name", "What is seen"], [
   ["New Moon", "Not visible"], ["Waxing crescent", "Thin bright curve growing"], ["First quarter (half)", "Half circle, waxing"],
   ["Waxing gibbous", "More than half bright"], ["Full Moon", "Complete bright circle"], ["Waning gibbous", "More than half, shrinking"],
   ["Last quarter (half)", "Half circle, waning"], ["Waning crescent", "Thin curve shrinking"]]),
 ("h3", "11.1.2 Locating the Moon"),
 ("ul", ["On **full Moon**, the Moon is nearly **opposite the Sun**: when the Sun rises in the east, the Moon is nearly setting in the west.",
         "As the bright part decreases the Moon appears **closer to the Sun** each morning; at **half Moon (waning) it is overhead at sunrise**; the crescent is even closer to the Sun.",
         "A **waxing Moon is easiest to see at sunset**, and a **waning Moon at sunrise**.",
         "The Moon rises about **50 minutes later each day**. It does not always rise when the Sun sets; sometimes moonrise is in the afternoon (2–4 p.m.), so the Moon can be seen in the eastern sky in daylight (wait ~30 minutes after the listed time)."]),
 ("h3", "11.1.3 Why do phases occur?"),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "The Moon’s **shape does not change**; only the part we see changes. The Moon does not emit light but **reflects sunlight**; the half **facing the Sun is illuminated**, the other half is dark. The Moon revolves around Earth in about a month and **always shows the same half to Earth**, but that half is not always the illuminated half. The change in the **fraction of the illuminated portion seen from Earth** causes the phases."),
 ("steps", [
   ("Activity 11.2 — Ball and lamp model", "In a dark place, a torch/lamp (~3 m away) represents the Sun, your head the Earth, a ball on a stick the Moon. Hold the ball at arm’s length slightly above your head and turn slowly anticlockwise."),
   ("Observation", "Ball opposite the lamp (A): whole lit part faces you = **full Moon**. Ball toward the lamp (E): only the dark side faces you = **new Moon**. In between, the dividing line is curved, as on the Moon."),
   ("Mapping to the orbit", "Positions B and H: more than half of the lit part visible = **gibbous**; D and F: less than half = **crescent**; C and G: half visible. A→C→E is the **waning** side, E→G→A is **waxing**.")]),
 ("ul", ["Earth’s rotation period (one day) is much smaller than the Moon’s revolution (~a month), so people everywhere see **nearly the same phase** on a given day.",
         "New Moon: Moon appears closest to the Sun; full Moon: farthest.",
         "**Why 50 minutes later?** In one day the Moon moves a bit further in its orbit, so Earth must rotate ~50 minutes more for the Moon to be at the same spot in the sky."]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "**Misconception:** Earth’s shadow does **not** cause phases; Earth’s shadow on the Moon causes a **lunar eclipse**. Lunar eclipses occur only on **full Moon**, solar eclipses only on **new Moon**, but not every month because the **Moon’s orbit is slightly tilted** relative to Earth’s orbit around the Sun."),
 ("h2", "11.2 How Did Calendars Come into Existence?"),
 ("p", "Natural periodic events — day and night, Moon phases, seasons — are used to keep time. The Sun’s daily rise in the east and set in the west is due to **Earth’s rotation** and gives the unit **day**. The average time between the Sun’s highest position on two successive days is the **mean solar day = 24 hours**."),
 ("steps", [
   ("Activity 11.3 — Measure a day with a shadow", "Fix a 1 m stick vertically on flat ground in sunlight. From ~11:00 a.m. to ~1:10 p.m. mark the tip of the shadow every minute. The **shadow is shortest when the Sun is highest**; note that time. Repeat next day: the difference between times is the solar day (e.g. 12:20 to 12:20 = 24:00; next day 12:19 = 23:59). The average is nearly 24 hours.")]),
 ("table", ["Time unit", "Natural cycle", "Duration"], [
   ["**Day**", "Sun returning to its highest position (Earth’s **rotation**)", "24 hours"],
   ["**Month**", "Cycle of Moon’s phases (Moon’s **revolution** around Earth)", "≈ 29.5 days"],
   ["**Year**", "Cycle of seasons (Earth’s **revolution** around the Sun)", "≈ 365¼ days"]]),
 ("h3", "11.2.1 Lunar calendars"),
 ("ul", ["About **12 lunar months** fit in one cycle of seasons, giving a lunar calendar: shortest unit a day, month ≈ 29.5 days, **lunar year = 12 months = 354 days**.",
         "Easy to track by watching the Moon, but seasons **do not stay aligned** with the same lunar months each year because the seasons repeat in ~365 days."]),
 ("h3", "11.2.2 Solar calendars"),
 ("ul", ["Farmers needed to know the seasons, so solar calendars keep the year in step with them. The **Gregorian calendar** is a solar calendar.",
         "Months are adjusted to add to **365 days** (30- and 31-day months; February 28).",
         "Earth takes about an **extra quarter day**; these add up to one day every four years, so a **leap year** with a **29-day February** is added when the year is divisible by 4."]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "**Fine corrections:** Earth’s year is slightly less than 365¼ days, so century years (1700, 1800, 1900) are **not** leap years, but a year divisible by **400** (1600, 2000) is a leap year again. The Gregorian calendar follows the **tropical year** (successive spring equinoxes); the **sidereal year** (same stars rising at sunset) is longer by about **20 minutes** and is used by astronomers to track Earth’s position in its orbit."),
 ("p", "**Heritage:** ancient observers, without knowing Earth goes round the Sun, found the year ≈ 365 days. The Sun rises a little north of east in summer and a little south of east in winter, the extremes being the **solstices** (~21 June and 21 December). The Sun’s apparent northward movement (December to June) is **Uttarayan**, southward (June to December) is **Dakshinayan**. The Taittirīya Saṁhitā (6.5.3) states the Sun moves south for six months and north for six months, and the Surya Siddhanta ties these to the Sun’s entry into Makar (Capricorn) and Karka (Cancer). On equinoxes the Sun rises exactly east and sets exactly west."),
 ("h3", "11.2.3 Luni-solar calendars"),
 ("ul", ["They count days and months by the Moon’s phases but adjust to stay in step with seasons.",
         "12 lunar months = 354 days, **about 11 days short** of the solar year; in 2–3 years the gap is nearly a month, so an extra month, **Adhika Maasa (intercalary month)**, is added every few years.",
         "Months: Chaitra, Vaisakha, Jyeshtha, Ashadha, Shravana, Bhadrapada, Ashwin, Kartika, Margashirsha, Pausha, Magha, Phalguna.",
         "**Amant** calendars: month starts after new Moon, ends on new Moon. **Purnimant**: month starts after full Moon, ends on full Moon."]),
 ("h3", "11.2.4 The Indian National Calendar"),
 ("ul", ["A **solar** calendar of **365 days**, used with the Gregorian calendar for official purposes; year starts on **22 March** (day after spring equinox).",
         "Months have 30 or 31 days: in a regular year months 2–6 have 31 days and the rest 30; names from traditional Indian calendars (first month **Chaitra**).",
         "In leap years a day is added to Chaitra and the year begins on 21 March.",
         "The **Calendar Reform Committee** (1952) recommended it; adopted **21 March 1956** (1 Chaitra 1878 Saka); follows Surya Siddhanta principles.",
         "**Meghnad Saha** (1893–1956), astrophysicist known for the Saha equation, chaired the committee; the Saha Institute of Nuclear Physics, Kolkata, is named after him."]),
 ("table", ["Calendar", "Based on", "Features", "Example"], [
   ["Lunar", "Moon phases", "354-day year; seasons drift", "Islamic calendar (Eid-ul-Fitr)"],
   ["Solar", "Earth’s revolution / seasons", "365 days + leap year", "Gregorian, Indian National Calendar"],
   ["Luni-solar", "Moon phases + seasons", "Intercalary (Adhika) month", "Many Indian regional calendars"]]),
 ("h2", "11.3 Are Festivals Related to Astronomical Phenomena?"),
 ("ul", ["Many festivals follow Moon phases: **Diwali** on the new Moon of Kartika, **Holi** on the full Moon of Phalguna, **Buddha Purnima** on the full Moon of Vaisakha, **Dussehra** on the 10th day of Ashwina; **Eid-ul-Fitr** is celebrated after sighting the **crescent Moon** at the end of Ramazan.",
         "They fall on different Gregorian dates each year. Luni-solar festivals shift by **less than a month** because of the intercalary month; purely lunar festivals like Eid move through all months (about **11 days earlier** each year).",
         "**Makar Sankranti, Pongal, Bihu, Vaisakhi, Poila Baisakh, Puthandu** follow a **solar sidereal** calendar and fall on almost the same Gregorian date; slow wobble of Earth’s axis shifts them gradually (Makar Sankranti moves one day every 71 years).",
         "Festival dates depend on lunar phase at sunrise, which differs by a day between east and west India; the **Positional Astronomy Centre** publishes the **Rashtriya Panchang** for uniform dates.",
         "**Culture:** Moon imagery appears in ragas (Chandrakauns, Chandranandan), Bharatanatyam mudras (Chandrakala, Ardhachandran), Madhubani and Warli paintings, Dhokra art."]),
 ("h2", "11.4 Why Do We Launch Artificial Satellites?"),
 ("ul", ["The Moon is Earth’s **natural satellite**; **artificial satellites** are human-made and orbit Earth, appearing as fast-moving specks. Most orbit ~**800 km** above Earth and take about **100 minutes** per orbit — also a periodic motion.",
         "Uses: **communication, navigation, weather monitoring, disaster management, scientific research**.",
         "ISRO: **Cartosat** (images for maps and city planning; **Bhuvan** platform), **AstroSat** (observes stars), Chandrayaan 1, 2, 3 (Moon), Aditya L1 (Sun), Mangalyaan (Mars); student satellites AzaadiSat, InspireSat-1, Jugnu.",
         "**Activity 11.4:** spot satellites just before sunrise or after sunset (with an adult, clear sky): a steady or flickering point of light moving fast; apps help predict passes.",
         "**Space debris:** dead satellites and rocket parts crowd space and may collide with working satellites; small pieces burn in the atmosphere, large ones can reach the ground.",
         "**Vikram Sarabhai** (1919–1971), Father of the Indian Space Programme; Vikram Sarabhai Space Centre (Thiruvananthapuram) develops rockets and launch vehicles."]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "**Tides:** sea level rises and falls in a regular pattern; a given tide returns about **50 minutes later each day**, like moonrise, showing tides are linked to the Moon’s position and phase. The crescent’s bright side always faces the Sun."),
 ("h2", "Quick Questions with Model Answers"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What causes the phases of the Moon?", "As the Moon revolves around Earth, we see different fractions of its sunlit half."),
  ("Do Earth’s shadows cause Moon phases?", "No; Earth’s shadow causes a lunar eclipse."),
  ("Differentiate waxing and waning, Shukla and Krishna Paksha.", "Waxing: bright part grows (Shukla Paksha); waning: it shrinks (Krishna Paksha)."),
  ("When is a waxing Moon best seen? A waning Moon?", "Waxing at sunset; waning at sunrise."),
  ("Why does the Moon rise ~50 minutes later each day?", "It moves ahead in its orbit, so Earth must rotate further."),
  ("Why don’t eclipses occur every month?", "The Moon’s orbit is slightly tilted to Earth’s orbit."),
  ("On what natural cycle are day, month and year based?", "Earth’s rotation, Moon’s phases (revolution), and the cycle of seasons (Earth’s revolution)."),
  ("Why is a leap year needed?", "Earth takes about an extra quarter day; four years add one day."),
  ("Why do lunar-calendar festivals fall on different dates?", "A lunar year has only 354 days; luni-solar calendars add an intercalary month."),
  ("What is the Indian National Calendar?", "A 365-day solar calendar starting 22 March, adopted 21 March 1956."),
  ("Give uses of artificial satellites.", "Communication, navigation, weather monitoring, disaster management and research."),
  ("Amol was born on a full Moon day on 6 May. Is his birthday on full Moon every year?", "No; the Moon cycle (29.5 days) does not match the Gregorian calendar.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["The Moon shines by **reflected sunlight**; its shape is constant, but the lit fraction we see changes — **phases** (~29.5 days).",
  "Full Moon is opposite the Sun; new Moon is invisible; **waxing** = Shukla Paksha, **waning** = Krishna Paksha.",
  "Moonrise is ~**50 minutes later daily**; Earth’s shadow gives eclipses, not phases.",
  "**Day** (rotation), **month** (Moon’s phases), **year** (seasons) are the basic time units.",
  "**Lunar** (354 days), **solar** (365 days + leap year) and **luni-solar** (with Adhika Maasa) calendars; Indian National Calendar starts 22 March.",
  "Festivals follow lunar, luni-solar or solar-sidereal calendars; **artificial satellites** serve communication, navigation, weather and research."])
